A||||10||Red2m4||Belt Problems - Need Advice||||1||
Z||000000||Red2m4||10-05-2001||06:27 PM||||<BR>I've been having a bad fiero day...<P>It was squeeling bad today, ok, i thought, will have to tighten it.  Had to get to work, and driving to work the battery light came on...no squeeling though.  I got there and the belt had come off.  I tried to pull it, but it wouldnt come out.  I'm assuming it aint broke.<P>So, now i have it in the parking lot.  Of couse, I'm an hour from home.  So, should I try and fix this myself?  I could drive it to the shop...I think.  If not, I got AAA.<P>Of couse this happens when it's cold and rainy in Buffalo.<P>Well...should I get some one else to do this?  Or should I pay someone else to?  I'd have to make this repair in the parking lot, in the rain.  I have very few tools with me.||||reg||1
Z||000001||WampusCat||10-05-2001||07:22 PM||||Don't know about the 2M4 but on the V6, I tighten the belt by jacking the car up and removing the right rear wheel.  You can get a big screwdriver under the alternator and get to the bolt easily from there.  Not something I would want to do in a rainy parking lot though.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||10-05-2001||07:53 PM||||Call  AAA and get it towed to someplace to replace the belts.  I had mine done at the Pontiac place on Ridge Road west in Rochester.  The belts were pricy little devils and Ed, the mechanic, said that it was a PITA to replace the belts.  But he had the car on a lift and was able to go through the wheel well.  He should have he technique down pat, I paid for the training.  <P>I had replced mine after the sqealing started.  I didn't want a belt to break at an inoppertune time, like a dark rainy night.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Robert||10-05-2001||07:56 PM||||My belt squeals anytime it rains, isn't that normal?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierowrecker||10-05-2001||11:42 PM||||Hey Red!<BR>Check the alternator first for an elongated hole where the long bolt goes...<BR>If it is still round, skip it, but if it is oval instead of round, replace the alternator...<BR>Then clean the pulleys and install a new GATES belt...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jake_Dragon||10-06-2001||07:11 AM||||Was tightening my belt the other day on the 2m4 and the adjustment bold striped, replaced it with a 3/8 bold and nut. Hasn't come loose yet. Use a lock washer and lock nut if you have them. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||David Bartlett||10-06-2001||03:01 PM||||I tightened mine the other day... It was easy just drive one side of your car up onto a curb and slide under the car, have someone tighten the slack and you tighten the bottom bolt.  I did it in the rain in 5 minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Back On Holiday||10-06-2001||03:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by David Bartlett:<BR><B>I tightened mine the other day... It was easy just drive one side of your car up onto a curb and slide under the car, have someone tighten the slack and you tighten the bottom bolt.  I did it in the rain in 5 minutes.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I'll have to remember that trick in an emergency  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Red2m4||10-06-2001||05:19 PM||||<BR>Well, I bit the bullet and paid to have the belts replaced.  It cost $190, they replaced all three.  But, it has a 1 yr. parts an labor warranty.  The belts were badly cracked.  Prob the original on the car.  At home, where my tools are, i might have done it myself.  <P>well, at least it's done...<BR>even though i had to borrow the $ from a friend.||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||10-06-2001||06:13 PM|||| [QUOTE]Originally posted by Red2m4:<BR>[b]<BR>Well, I bit the bullet and paid to have the belts replaced.  It cost $190, they replaced all three.  But, it has a 1 yr. parts an labor warranty.  The belts were badly cracked.  Prob the original on the car.  At home, where my tools are, i might have done it myself.  <BR>[quote}<P>DAMN!!!!!!! I have a V6, $20 serpentine belt and a cheaper AC belt. I can get a clutch installed for not much more than that !! I think you got ripped royal. But then I guess youll pay whatever it takes in an emergency.<P>any yes, mine squeals a little when i drive thru deeper puddles in rain, but grabs back in a few seconds.<P><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 10-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||William Federle||10-06-2001||08:33 PM||||I have a 84 Fiero and just have one belt since my AC doesn't work. I keep a pry bar with me in the trunk as well as the wrench I need to loosen the alternator. I also keep a spare belt. I wouldn't be so paranoid about it but all I can find are those darned cog belts. Once they start squealing they're bound to break within a short amount of time. The cogs seem to break off. I wish I knew where to find one of those Gates belts.<P>My advice is, keep tools to change the belts with you and also a spare alternator belt. <P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 10-06-2001).]||||reg||1
